window.QUESTIONS = window.QUESTIONS || [];
window.QUESTIONS.push(
  {"id":"q-d11-01","day":11,"lesson":"d11-coag","topic":"Hemostasis","stem":"<p>Six hours after CABG, a patient has diffuse microvascular bleeding. ACT is 176 seconds, and a paired viscoelastic heparinase channel corrects the prolonged clot-initiation time. Platelet count is 118 × 10⁹/L, fibrinogen is 2.1 g/L, and no focal source is found. Which intervention is most appropriate first?</p>","options":["Give a small additional dose of protamine","Transfuse one adult dose of platelets","Administer four-factor prothrombin complex concentrate","Return immediately to bypass for surgical inspection"],"answer":0,"explain":"<p>Correction in the heparinase channel supports residual heparin effect rather than nonspecific ACT prolongation, so a carefully titrated additional protamine dose is appropriate. ACT alone can be prolonged by hypothermia, hemodilution, and platelet dysfunction; empiric excess protamine can itself worsen coagulation.</p>"},
  {"id":"q-d11-02","day":11,"lesson":"d11-coag","topic":"Hemostasis","stem":"<p>A patient receiving unfractionated heparin for five days before valve surgery has a platelet fall from 240 to 88 × 10⁹/L and a new femoral DVT. What is the best immediate management?</p>","options":["Continue heparin and send a PF4 antibody assay","Stop all heparin and begin a non-heparin anticoagulant","Give platelets and withhold all anticoagulation","Reverse heparin with protamine and begin warfarin alone"],"answer":1,"explain":"<p>The platelet fall and new thrombosis give a high clinical probability of HIT. Heparin must be stopped and therapeutic argatroban or bivalirudin begun while testing proceeds; platelet transfusion does not treat the prothrombotic process.</p>"},
  {"id":"q-d11-03","day":11,"lesson":"d11-coag","topic":"Hemostasis","stem":"<p>During CPB, the ACT remains 330 seconds after adequate weight-based heparin. Antithrombin activity is markedly reduced. Which treatment most directly permits safe initiation of bypass?</p>","options":["Administer tranexamic acid before repeating the ACT","Give protamine to improve heparin responsiveness","Replace antithrombin with concentrate or plasma","Proceed because an ACT above 300 seconds is adequate"],"answer":2,"explain":"<p>Heparin requires antithrombin to inhibit thrombin, so antithrombin deficiency causes heparin resistance. Antithrombin concentrate is targeted; plasma is an alternative. More heparin alone may not produce a reliable anticoagulant effect.</p>"},
  {"id":"q-d11-04","day":11,"lesson":"d11-coag","topic":"Hemostasis","level":5,"kind":"complication-rescue","stem":"<p>Ten minutes after protamine administration, a patient develops abrupt pulmonary hypertension, severe RV dilation, and systemic hypotension. Which action best addresses this complication?</p>","options":["Give another full protamine dose to neutralize rebound heparin","Rapidly transfuse plasma to restore coagulation factors","Administer desmopressin for platelet dysfunction","Stop protamine and support the right ventricle"],"answer":3,"explain":"<p>This is a severe protamine reaction with acute pulmonary vasoconstriction and RV failure. Stop the exposure and support oxygenation, RV perfusion, and pulmonary vasodilation; additional protamine would intensify the reaction.</p>"},
  {"id":"q-d11-05","day":11,"lesson":"d11-coag","topic":"Hemostasis","stem":"<p>A patient takes aspirin until urgent CABG. Post-bypass bleeding is diffuse, platelet count is 165 × 10⁹/L, ACT is normal, fibrin-specific clot strength is preserved, and whole-clot strength plus ADP/arachidonic-acid platelet testing are markedly impaired. What therapy best targets the demonstrated deficit?</p>","options":["Transfuse platelets for qualitative platelet dysfunction","Give additional protamine for occult heparin effect","Administer cryoprecipitate despite normal fibrinogen","Observe because a normal platelet count excludes dysfunction"],"answer":0,"explain":"<p>Preserved fibrin contribution with low whole-clot strength and abnormal platelet-function testing identifies qualitative platelet dysfunction despite a normal count. Platelet transfusion directly supplies functioning platelets. Without the fibrin-specific and platelet-function findings, low whole-clot strength alone would not distinguish platelet dysfunction from low fibrinogen.</p>"},
  {"id":"q-d11-06","day":11,"lesson":"d11-coag","topic":"Hemostasis","stem":"<p>After complex aortic surgery, rotational thromboelastometry shows a low FIBTEM amplitude while EXTEM clot firmness is also reduced. Which product best targets the measured deficit?</p>","options":["Fresh frozen plasma for isolated factor replacement","Fibrinogen concentrate or cryoprecipitate","Platelets because all low clot firmness is platelet-mediated","Packed red cells to improve clot polymerization"],"answer":1,"explain":"<p>A low FIBTEM amplitude identifies inadequate fibrin-based clot strength, usually from hypofibrinogenemia. Cryoprecipitate or fibrinogen concentrate directly replaces fibrinogen; plasma is less concentrated and slower for this specific deficit.</p>"},
  {"id":"q-d11-07","day":11,"lesson":"d11-coag","topic":"Hemostasis","stem":"<p>A stable patient after CABG has hemoglobin 72 g/L, no active ischemia, normal lactate, and minimal chest-tube drainage. Which transfusion strategy is most appropriate?</p>","options":["Transfuse to a hemoglobin above 100 g/L for every cardiac patient","Give two units immediately because bypass mandates liberal transfusion","Use a restrictive strategy and assess symptoms before one unit","Avoid transfusion even if tissue hypoxia subsequently develops"],"answer":2,"explain":"<p>In a stable postoperative patient without active ischemia, a restrictive threshold and single-unit reassessment reduces unnecessary exposure. A universal target above 100 g/L offers no routine benefit and increases transfusion complications.</p>"},
  {"id":"q-d11-08","day":11,"lesson":"d11-coag","topic":"Hemostasis","stem":"<p>A bleeding patient after CPB has rapid clot breakdown on viscoelastic testing, while clot initiation, platelet contribution, and fibrin-based clot strength are adequate. Which intervention most directly targets this pattern?</p>","options":["Additional protamine for proven residual heparin","Platelets for an isolated count deficit","Four-factor PCC to increase thrombin generation","An antifibrinolytic such as tranexamic acid"],"answer":3,"explain":"<p>Rapid breakdown of an initially adequate clot indicates hyperfibrinolysis. Tranexamic acid inhibits plasminogen activation and stabilizes fibrin; protamine, platelets, and PCC target different defects and should not be given solely for this tracing.</p>"},
  {"id":"q-d11-09","day":11,"lesson":"d11-bleeding","topic":"Hemostasis","stem":"<p>One hour after CABG, chest-tube output is 320 mL followed by 280 mL the next hour. Coagulation tests and viscoelastic parameters are normal. What is the best next step?</p>","options":["Prompt surgical reassessment for a focal bleeding source","Empiric plasma and platelet transfusion before examination","Wait six hours because early drainage is expected","Give high-dose tranexamic acid regardless of thrombosis risk"],"answer":0,"explain":"<p>High sustained drainage with normal coagulation points toward surgical bleeding rather than diffuse coagulopathy. Early re-exploration should be considered; empiric blood products delay control and expose the patient to harm.</p>"},
  {"id":"q-d11-10","day":11,"lesson":"d11-bleeding","topic":"Hemostasis","stem":"<p>After valve surgery, chest-tube output is brisk and diffuse. ACT is normal, fibrinogen is adequate, but platelet count is 42 × 10⁹/L. Which treatment best matches the abnormality?</p>","options":["Cryoprecipitate to raise factor VIII and fibrinogen","An adult therapeutic dose of platelets","Protamine because thrombocytopenia prolongs ACT","PCC because it replaces platelet phospholipid"],"answer":1,"explain":"<p>Severe thrombocytopenia with active bleeding requires platelet replacement. Cryoprecipitate treats fibrinogen deficiency, and PCC supplies coagulation factors rather than platelets.</p>"},
  {"id":"q-d11-11","day":11,"lesson":"d11-bleeding","topic":"Hemostasis","stem":"<p>A patient on warfarin requires emergency repair of an acute type A dissection. INR is 3.8 and there is no time to wait for vitamin K alone. What is the fastest reversal strategy?</p>","options":["Tranexamic acid alone followed by immediate incision","Platelets plus desmopressin before induction","Four-factor PCC plus intravenous vitamin K","Subcutaneous vitamin K and recheck the INR tomorrow"],"answer":2,"explain":"<p>Four-factor PCC rapidly replaces vitamin K–dependent factors, while IV vitamin K sustains reversal after PCC wears off. Vitamin K alone is too slow for emergency surgery, and platelets do not reverse warfarin.</p>"},
  {"id":"q-d11-12","day":11,"lesson":"d11-bleeding","topic":"Hemostasis","stem":"<p>A patient with stage 5 kidney disease has persistent oozing after sternotomy despite normal platelet count, PT, fibrinogen, and ACT. Which therapy can rapidly improve the suspected defect?</p>","options":["Protamine for occult heparin rebound","PCC for isolated factor XIII deficiency","Red-cell transfusion to normalize the platelet count","Desmopressin for uremic platelet dysfunction"],"answer":3,"explain":"<p>Uremia causes qualitative platelet dysfunction despite a normal count and routine coagulation tests. Desmopressin transiently improves platelet adhesion by releasing von Willebrand factor; protamine would not correct this defect.</p>"},
  {"id":"q-d11-13","day":11,"lesson":"d11-bleeding","topic":"Hemostasis","stem":"<p>A postoperative patient has diffuse bleeding, prolonged PT and aPTT, normal fibrinogen, normal platelet count, and no residual heparin on anti-Xa testing. Which product is the most appropriate replacement?</p>","options":["Plasma to replace multiple depleted coagulation factors","Platelets because both PT and aPTT are prolonged","Cryoprecipitate for isolated fibrinogen deficiency","Packed red cells because anemia prolongs coagulation times"],"answer":0,"explain":"<p>Concurrent PT and aPTT prolongation with adequate fibrinogen and platelets suggests broad factor depletion, appropriately treated with plasma. Cryoprecipitate is targeted mainly to low fibrinogen.</p>"},
  {"id":"q-d11-14","day":11,"lesson":"d11-bleeding","topic":"Hemostasis","stem":"<p>During massive bleeding after aortic surgery, ionized calcium falls to 0.82 mmol/L and hypotension worsens. What is the most appropriate response?</p>","options":["Stop all blood products because citrate toxicity is irreversible","Give intravenous calcium during goal-directed resuscitation","Give sodium bicarbonate as the sole treatment","Increase protamine because hypocalcemia indicates heparin rebound"],"answer":1,"explain":"<p>Citrate in transfused products binds calcium, impairing vascular tone, contractility, and coagulation. IV calcium with repeat measurements is appropriate; stopping necessary hemorrhage resuscitation is not.</p>"},
  {"id":"q-d11-15","day":11,"lesson":"d11-bleeding","topic":"Hemostasis","stem":"<p>A patient with major postoperative hemorrhage is cold at 33.5°C and acidemic with pH 7.18. Why should active warming and correction of perfusion accompany product therapy?</p>","options":["Hypothermia affects only platelet count, while acidosis improves enzymes","Warming prevents heparin from binding antithrombin","Hypothermia and acidosis impair platelets and coagulation enzymes","Temperature correction replaces the need for surgical hemostasis"],"answer":2,"explain":"<p>Hypothermia and acidosis worsen enzymatic coagulation and platelet function, creating a self-reinforcing bleeding cycle. Blood products alone cannot reliably restore hemostasis until physiology and the bleeding source are addressed.</p>"},
  {"id":"q-d11-16","day":11,"lesson":"d11-bleeding","topic":"Hemostasis","stem":"<p>An asymptomatic patient develops an isolated temperature rise from 36.8°C to 38.2°C during a red-cell transfusion. Blood pressure and urine output are unchanged. What is the first action?</p>","options":["Continue the unit because fever is expected after bypass","Give antibiotics through the same line and continue transfusion","Administer protamine for a febrile heparin reaction","Stop the transfusion and begin a reaction evaluation"],"answer":3,"explain":"<p>Any suspected transfusion reaction requires immediate cessation and clerical/hemolysis evaluation while maintaining IV access with saline. Continuing the unit risks worsening an acute hemolytic or septic reaction.</p>"},
  {"id":"q-d11-17","day":11,"lesson":"d11-bleeding","topic":"Hemostasis","stem":"<p>Minutes after red-cell transfusion begins, a patient develops hypotension, flank pain, hemoglobinuria, and diffuse bleeding. Which diagnosis and response are most appropriate?</p>","options":["Acute hemolytic reaction; stop transfusion and support renal perfusion","Febrile nonhemolytic reaction; slow the transfusion rate","TRALI; give aggressive diuresis as first-line therapy","TACO; resume transfusion after acetaminophen"],"answer":0,"explain":"<p>Hypotension, pain, hemoglobinuria, and DIC suggest acute intravascular hemolysis, often from ABO incompatibility. Stop transfusion immediately and support perfusion; a febrile reaction does not cause hemoglobinuria.</p>"},
  {"id":"q-d11-18","day":11,"lesson":"d11-bleeding","topic":"Hemostasis","stem":"<p>Two hours after plasma transfusion, a patient develops severe hypoxemia and bilateral infiltrates with no JVP elevation and normal cardiac filling pressures. What is the most likely diagnosis?</p>","options":["TACO from hydrostatic pulmonary edema","TRALI from noncardiogenic permeability edema","Anaphylaxis limited to the upper airway","Hemolysis causing isolated pulmonary hypertension"],"answer":1,"explain":"<p>Acute hypoxemia and bilateral infiltrates within six hours of transfusion without circulatory overload indicate TRALI. TACO usually features elevated filling pressures, JVP, and response to diuresis.</p>"},
  {"id":"q-d11-19","day":11,"lesson":"d11-bleeding","topic":"Hemostasis","stem":"<p>A patient with CKD develops dyspnea, hypertension, elevated JVP, and pulmonary edema during rapid transfusion. Which management is most appropriate?</p>","options":["More plasma to correct dilutional coagulopathy","Epinephrine for presumed anaphylaxis","Stop transfusion, give oxygen and diuresis for TACO","Platelet transfusion to improve pulmonary capillary integrity"],"answer":2,"explain":"<p>Hypertension, elevated JVP, and volume-sensitive CKD identify transfusion-associated circulatory overload. Stop transfusion and treat hydrostatic congestion; TRALI is typically not associated with high filling pressures.</p>"},
  {"id":"q-d11-20","day":11,"lesson":"d11-bleeding","topic":"Hemostasis","stem":"<p>A patient with known severe IgA deficiency develops wheezing, hypotension, and urticaria immediately after plasma starts. What is the best immediate treatment?</p>","options":["Continue slowly after an antihistamine only","Administer furosemide for volume overload","Give cryoprecipitate because it contains less citrate","Stop transfusion and give intramuscular epinephrine"],"answer":3,"explain":"<p>This is transfusion-associated anaphylaxis, requiring immediate cessation and epinephrine. Diuresis treats TACO, which causes congestion rather than urticaria and bronchospasm.</p>"},
  {"id":"q-d11-21","day":11,"lesson":"d11-reexplore","topic":"Hemostasis","level":4,"kind":"complication-rescue","stem":"<p>Three hours after CABG, drainage abruptly stops, CVP rises, pulse pressure narrows, and the patient becomes oliguric. What is the most appropriate next step?</p>","options":["Obtain urgent bedside imaging and prepare for re-exploration","Record the decreased drainage as successful hemostasis","Give a large fluid bolus and reassess the next morning","Remove all chest tubes before obtaining echocardiography"],"answer":0,"explain":"<p>Abruptly absent drainage with rising CVP and shock suggests clotted tubes and tamponade, not cessation of bleeding. Urgent assessment and operative decompression may be lifesaving; fluids only temporize.</p>"},
  {"id":"q-d11-22","day":11,"lesson":"d11-reexplore","topic":"Hemostasis","stem":"<p>Diffuse life-threatening bleeding continues after a surgical source has been excluded and temperature, pH, calcium, platelets, fibrinogen, residual heparin, and coagulation-factor deficits have all been corrected. What is the most appropriate role for recombinant factor VIIa?</p>","options":["Give it routinely before targeted correction of coagulation deficits","Consider it only as rescue therapy after multidisciplinary reassessment","Use it to replace fibrinogen in every bleeding patient","Administer it before excluding tamponade or a focal surgical source"],"answer":1,"explain":"<p>Recombinant factor VIIa carries important arterial and venous thrombotic risk. It is not first-line therapy; selected use may be considered only for otherwise uncontrollable nonsurgical bleeding after correction of reversible physiologic and coagulation defects and explicit multidisciplinary risk-benefit review.</p>"},
  {"id":"q-d11-23","day":11,"lesson":"d11-reexplore","topic":"Hemostasis","stem":"<p>A post-CABG patient is hypotensive with localized posterior clot on TEE compressing the left atrium. There is no large circumferential effusion. What is the best interpretation?</p>","options":["Tamponade is excluded because the effusion is not circumferential","This must be isolated LV failure because the RV is not collapsed","Localized postoperative tamponade can occur and needs urgent surgical review","Pericardiocentesis is always definitive for organized posterior clot"],"answer":2,"explain":"<p>Postoperative tamponade is often localized by adhesions and may compress a single chamber without classic circumferential findings. Organized posterior clot is poorly treated by percutaneous drainage and warrants surgical evaluation.</p>"},
  {"id":"q-d11-24","day":11,"lesson":"d11-reexplore","topic":"Hemostasis","stem":"<p>After rewarming from bypass, a patient's ACT is normal but bleeding resumes two hours later and ACT becomes prolonged. Which mechanism is most likely?</p>","options":["New vitamin K deficiency developing within two hours","Irreversible aspirin effect appearing only after rewarming","Acute fibrinogen synthesis failure from protamine","Redistribution of heparin from tissues causing heparin rebound"],"answer":3,"explain":"<p>Heparin can redistribute from tissue compartments after initial protamine reversal, producing delayed ACT prolongation and bleeding. Targeted additional protamine may be used; platelet inhibition would not newly prolong ACT.</p>"},
  {"id":"q-d11-25","day":11,"lesson":"d11-reexplore","topic":"Hemostasis","stem":"<p>A patient has 180 mL/h chest drainage, falling hemoglobin, and a new unilateral white-out on chest radiograph while mediastinal tubes remain relatively dry. What should be suspected?</p>","options":["Retained hemothorax from an inadequately drained pleural source","Normal postoperative atelectasis requiring only incentive spirometry","TRALI despite blood confined to one hemithorax","Protamine-induced pulmonary hypertension without bleeding"],"answer":0,"explain":"<p>A unilateral opacity with blood loss and deceptively low mediastinal output suggests retained pleural blood or hemothorax. It requires drainage and source assessment; routine atelectasis does not explain the hemoglobin fall.</p>"},
  {"id":"q-d11-26","day":11,"lesson":"d11-reexplore","topic":"Hemostasis","stem":"<p>Following internal thoracic artery harvest, a patient develops persistent brisk left pleural drainage with otherwise normal coagulation. Which source is most likely?</p>","options":["Diffuse fibrinolysis from the CPB circuit","A surgical branch or chest-wall bleeding site","Heparin rebound despite a normal ACT","Isolated factor deficiency from one unit of plasma"],"answer":1,"explain":"<p>Localized brisk pleural bleeding with normal coagulation after conduit harvest points to a surgical branch or chest-wall source. Diffuse coagulopathy would produce generalized microvascular oozing.</p>"},
  {"id":"q-d11-27","day":11,"lesson":"d11-reexplore","topic":"Hemostasis","stem":"<p>A bleeding patient has normal PT, aPTT, platelet count, and fibrinogen but poor clot stability and delayed recurrent bleeding. Which uncommon deficiency should be considered?</p>","options":["Antithrombin deficiency causing excessive fibrin formation","Factor XII deficiency causing severe surgical hemorrhage","Factor XIII deficiency impairing fibrin cross-linking","Protein C deficiency causing isolated postoperative oozing"],"answer":2,"explain":"<p>Factor XIII cross-links fibrin after routine clotting endpoints, so deficiency can cause delayed bleeding with normal PT and aPTT. Factor XII deficiency may prolong aPTT but generally does not cause clinical bleeding.</p>"},
  {"id":"q-d11-28","day":11,"lesson":"d11-reexplore","topic":"Hemostasis","level":5,"kind":"when-not-to","stem":"<p>A patient given large empiric doses of PCC after CABG stops bleeding but develops acute graft thrombosis. What principle would most likely have prevented this complication?</p>","options":["Use PCC before checking for a surgical source","Target hemoglobin rather than coagulation defects","Combine PCC with routine recombinant factor VIIa","Use viscoelastic-guided, deficit-specific therapy"],"answer":3,"explain":"<p>Concentrated procoagulants can cause thrombosis when given without a demonstrated deficit. Goal-directed viscoelastic therapy limits unnecessary factor exposure; recombinant factor VIIa would increase thrombotic risk further.</p>"},
  {"id":"q-d11-29","day":11,"lesson":"d11-coag","topic":"Hemostasis","stem":"<p>A patient taking clopidogrel undergoes urgent CABG. Which preoperative finding best predicts increased bleeding risk from the medication?</p>","options":["Markedly impaired ADP-induced platelet aggregation","A prolonged INR with normal platelet function","A low fibrinogen concentration caused by P2Y12 blockade","An elevated ACT before any heparin is administered"],"answer":0,"explain":"<p>Clopidogrel blocks the platelet P2Y12 ADP receptor, so drug-specific platelet-function testing shows reduced ADP response. It does not directly prolong INR, lower fibrinogen, or raise baseline ACT.</p>"},
  {"id":"q-d11-30","day":11,"lesson":"d11-coag","topic":"Hemostasis","stem":"<p>A patient taking apixaban requires emergency cardiac surgery with imminent heparinization for CPB and has clinically significant drug activity. Which reversal approach is most appropriate?</p>","options":["Vitamin K because apixaban blocks vitamin K recycling","Use four-factor PCC according to the cardiac-surgery reversal protocol","Protamine because all anticoagulants bind heparin antagonists","Desmopressin because apixaban is primarily an antiplatelet drug"],"answer":1,"explain":"<p>For emergency cardiac surgery requiring imminent unfractionated heparin, many cardiac-surgery protocols use four-factor PCC for clinically important factor Xa inhibitor activity. Andexanet can bind heparin-antithrombin complexes, cause heparin resistance, and create thrombotic risk, so it should not be presented as interchangeable immediately before CPB. Vitamin K and protamine do not reverse apixaban.</p>"},
  {"id":"q-d11-31","day":11,"lesson":"d11-coag","topic":"Hemostasis","stem":"<p>During CPB, high-dose heparin produces an adequate ACT. Which laboratory measurement is most useful if profound hypothermia makes the ACT difficult to interpret?</p>","options":["Bleeding time as a measure of thrombin inhibition","INR as a direct measure of circulating heparin","Heparin concentration or anti-Xa–based assessment","D-dimer as proof that anticoagulation is adequate"],"answer":2,"explain":"<p>ACT is affected by hypothermia, hemodilution, and platelet dysfunction. Heparin concentration or anti-Xa assessment more directly estimates heparin effect; INR and D-dimer do not confirm safe bypass anticoagulation.</p>"},
  {"id":"q-d11-32","day":11,"lesson":"d11-coag","topic":"Hemostasis","stem":"<p>A patient receives excessive protamine after CPB and develops diffuse bleeding despite complete heparin neutralization. What is the likely mechanism?</p>","options":["Protamine activates vitamin K–dependent factor synthesis","Protamine raises fibrinogen beyond a functional range","Protamine causes delayed erythrocyte hemolysis only","Excess protamine itself impairs platelets and coagulation"],"answer":3,"explain":"<p>Protamine has anticoagulant effects when overdosed, including platelet and clotting impairment. Repeated empiric dosing can worsen bleeding; treatment should be guided by evidence of residual heparin.</p>"},
  {"id":"q-d11-33","day":11,"lesson":"d11-bleeding","topic":"Hemostasis","stem":"<p>A patient with active mediastinal bleeding receives tranexamic acid. Which mechanism explains its benefit?</p>","options":["It inhibits plasminogen activation and stabilizes formed fibrin","It directly replaces fibrinogen consumed during bypass","It reverses platelet P2Y12 receptor blockade","It neutralizes circulating unfractionated heparin"],"answer":0,"explain":"<p>Tranexamic acid is an antifibrinolytic lysine analogue that inhibits plasminogen binding and fibrin breakdown. It stabilizes clot but does not replace fibrinogen or reverse heparin.</p>"},
  {"id":"q-d11-34","day":11,"lesson":"d11-bleeding","topic":"Hemostasis","stem":"<p>A postoperative patient has falling platelets, schistocytes, renal injury, and prolonged PT and aPTT with low fibrinogen. Which diagnosis best explains the pattern?</p>","options":["Isolated HIT without another coagulation disorder","Disseminated intravascular coagulation","Uremic platelet dysfunction alone","Factor XIII deficiency after bypass"],"answer":1,"explain":"<p>Thrombocytopenia, microangiopathic hemolysis, factor consumption, and low fibrinogen indicate DIC. HIT is prothrombotic but typically does not itself prolong PT/aPTT or consume fibrinogen this way.</p>"},
  {"id":"q-d11-35","day":11,"lesson":"d11-reexplore","topic":"Hemostasis","stem":"<p>A patient has moderate early drainage that steadily declines, stable hemoglobin, warming physiology, and improving coagulation studies. Which management is most appropriate?</p>","options":["Re-explore immediately based on any postoperative drainage","Clamp the tubes to accelerate clot formation","Continue close observation with serial trends","Give empiric PCC to ensure drainage reaches zero"],"answer":2,"explain":"<p>A steadily improving trend with stable perfusion and laboratory correction supports careful observation. Re-exploration decisions use rate, trajectory, physiology, and correctable deficits rather than any drainage alone.</p>"}
);

/* Goal-directed hemostasis top-ups; Day-14 cross-links remain part of each lesson's final count. */
(() => {
  const rows = [
    ["d11-coag","During CPB, an ACT that had been above the institutional target falls sharply despite unchanged heparin dosing. The circuit is warming, venous-reservoir clot is not visible, and the sample was drawn correctly. What should occur next?",["Pause progression, repeat the measurement, assess heparin concentration or anti-Xa data, and restore verified anticoagulation","Continue bypass because one earlier adequate ACT guarantees anticoagulation for the entire case","Give protamine to determine whether the low ACT reflects excess heparin","Transfuse platelets as the only response because ACT measures platelet count"],0,"Safe CPB requires maintained, not merely initial, anticoagulation. An unexpected ACT fall should be confirmed rapidly while heparin delivery, sampling, antithrombin availability, temperature, and a complementary heparin measurement are assessed. Additional heparin is given according to the verified deficit and protocol before proceeding. Protamine moves in the wrong direction, while platelets do not substitute for adequate thrombin inhibition.","complication-rescue"],
    ["d11-coag","A patient with confirmed acute HIT requires emergency aortic surgery that cannot wait for antibody clearance. The team plans bivalirudin anticoagulation for CPB. Which circuit principle is essential?",["Avoid stagnant blood and follow an experienced bivalirudin-specific perfusion protocol","Use a heparin-coated circuit and add intermittent heparin boluses","Allow blood to remain motionless in the cardiotomy reservoir during circulatory arrest","Reverse bivalirudin completely with protamine before separation"],0,"Bivalirudin directly inhibits thrombin and is used when heparin is unsafe, but it has no specific reversal agent and is consumed in stagnant blood. Circuit design, dosing, monitoring, temperature planning, and continuous movement of blood must follow an experienced institutional protocol. Heparin exposure can reactivate HIT, deliberate stasis promotes clot, and protamine does not neutralize a direct thrombin inhibitor.","operative-step"],
    ["d11-coag","After a long CPB run, bleeding is diffuse. A heparinase viscoelastic channel normalizes clot initiation compared with the unmodified channel, while clot strength and fibrinolysis are satisfactory. What therapy is most targeted?",["A carefully titrated additional protamine dose","Platelet transfusion despite preserved clot strength","Cryoprecipitate despite preserved fibrin contribution","Recombinant factor VIIa before treating residual heparin"],0,"Correction in the heparinase channel isolates residual heparin as the dominant abnormality. A small, titrated protamine dose with repeat assessment directly addresses it while avoiding excess protamine, which can impair platelets and coagulation. Adequate clot strength argues against empiric platelets or fibrinogen. Bypass agents with thrombotic risk should not precede correction of a clearly demonstrated reversible cause.","reconcile-discordant"],
    ["d11-coag","Minutes after a small test dose of protamine, a patient develops flushing and systemic hypotension without pulmonary hypertension, bronchospasm, or RV dilation. Which reaction and treatment are most likely?",["A rate-related systemic hypotensive reaction; stop or slow protamine and support pressure","Acute pulmonary vasoconstriction requiring more rapid protamine infusion","Delayed heparin rebound requiring immediate full-dose protamine","HIT requiring platelet transfusion"],0,"Isolated vasodilatory hypotension during protamine exposure is most consistent with a type I, often rate-related reaction. Stop or slow the infusion, support arterial pressure, and exclude bleeding or another anesthetic cause before cautious reassessment. Abrupt pulmonary hypertension and RV failure define the more dangerous type III pattern. More rapid protamine can intensify hypotension rather than treat it.","complication-rescue"],
    ["d11-coag","A patient with prior protamine-containing insulin develops urticaria, bronchospasm, and hypotension immediately during protamine reversal. Pulmonary pressures are unchanged. What is the most appropriate response?",["Stop protamine and treat anaphylaxis, including epinephrine and airway support","Continue the infusion because pulmonary pressure is normal","Give more heparin as the sole treatment for bronchospasm","Administer tranexamic acid to reverse histamine release"],0,"Urticaria, bronchospasm, and systemic collapse identify an anaphylactic protamine reaction even without pulmonary vasoconstriction. Exposure should stop while epinephrine, oxygenation, airway management, fluids, and adjunctive therapy proceed. The team must subsequently balance incomplete heparin reversal against renewed exposure risk. Normal pulmonary pressure excludes neither anaphylaxis nor the need for immediate resuscitation.","dosed-algorithm"],
    ["d11-coag","A bleeding post-CPB patient has low whole-clot maximum firmness but normal fibrin-specific firmness. Platelet count is 105 × 10⁹/L and platelet-function testing is profoundly abnormal. What deficit is dominant?",["Qualitative platelet dysfunction","Isolated hypofibrinogenemia","Residual heparin effect alone","Primary hyperfibrinolysis"],0,"Whole-clot strength reflects platelets plus fibrin, whereas the fibrin-specific channel suppresses platelet contribution. Preserved fibrin firmness with weak whole-clot strength and abnormal function testing points to qualitative platelet failure, common after CPB and antiplatelet exposure. Residual heparin primarily prolongs initiation, and hyperfibrinolysis causes rapid clot breakdown rather than an isolated platelet contribution deficit.","reconcile-discordant"],
    ["d11-coag","After aortic surgery, viscoelastic testing shows adequate clot initiation and platelet contribution but markedly reduced fibrin-specific clot firmness. Fibrinogen is 1.1 g/L. Which replacement is most efficient?",["Fibrinogen concentrate or cryoprecipitate according to local protocol","Packed red cells because they contain concentrated fibrinogen","Protamine because low fibrin firmness proves residual heparin","Desmopressin because it directly replaces fibrinogen"],0,"The tracing and measured concentration agree on a fibrinogen deficit, often the first coagulation factor to become critically low during major cardiac bleeding. Cryoprecipitate or fibrinogen concentrate provides concentrated substrate and should be followed by repeat clinical and viscoelastic assessment. Red cells restore oxygen-carrying capacity, protamine neutralizes heparin, and desmopressin improves platelet adhesion rather than replacing fibrinogen.","complication-rescue"],
    ["d11-coag","Diffuse bleeding persists despite adequate platelets, fibrinogen, clot initiation, temperature, pH, calcium, and exclusion of a surgical source. Standard viscoelastic tests show a clot that forms but remains mechanically fragile. Which uncommon deficit should be considered?",["Factor XIII deficiency causing impaired fibrin cross-linking","Excess antithrombin causing heparin resistance","Aspirin effect causing isolated prolonged PT","Hypercalcemia preventing thrombin generation"],0,"Factor XIII cross-links fibrin after the clot has formed, so routine PT and aPTT can remain normal despite clinically important deficiency. It becomes relevant only after common physiologic, platelet, fibrinogen, residual-heparin, and surgical causes have been addressed. Targeted testing and replacement may then be appropriate. Aspirin affects platelets, while antithrombin deficiency produces heparin resistance rather than a fragile formed clot.","reconcile-discordant"],
    ["d11-coag","A post-CPB patient has life-threatening bleeding and a prolonged clot-initiation time from broad factor depletion. Fibrinogen and platelet contribution have already been corrected. When might four-factor PCC be considered?",["As carefully selected factor replacement after source control and thrombotic-risk review","As routine first-line treatment before assessing the surgical field","To replace platelets and fibrinogen simultaneously","To neutralize residual heparin without protamine"],0,"PCC rapidly supplies vitamin K–dependent factors in a small volume and may be useful for selected severe factor deficiency, especially when plasma volume is problematic. It does not replace fibrinogen or platelets and carries important thrombotic risk in fresh grafts and prostheses. The decision follows source control, correction of physiology, targeted testing, and multidisciplinary review rather than empiric early administration.","when-not-to"],
    ["d11-coag","A patient with severe uremia has diffuse mucosal and surgical-field oozing, a normal platelet count, and normal routine coagulation tests. Which mechanism explains a potential benefit from desmopressin?",["Release of endothelial von Willebrand factor improves platelet adhesion transiently","Direct replacement of vitamin K–dependent clotting factors","Neutralization of circulating heparin","Permanent correction of platelet production"],0,"Uremia produces qualitative platelet dysfunction, especially impaired adhesion, despite a normal count and PT/aPTT. Desmopressin releases von Willebrand factor and factor VIII from endothelial stores, producing a rapid but temporary improvement. It should be used selectively while dialysis, anemia, drugs, and surgical causes are addressed. It neither replaces multiple factors nor neutralizes heparin, and tachyphylaxis limits repeated dosing.","complication-rescue"],

    ["d11-bleeding","One hour after CABG, chest drainage remains brisk. The blood is bright red, a single pleural tube accounts for nearly all output, and coagulation testing is normal. What is the leading diagnosis?",["A focal surgical source in the pleural field","Diffuse factor depletion despite normal testing","Isolated hyperfibrinolysis confined to one tube","TACO from excessive transfusion"],0,"Bright localized drainage with normal coagulation is more consistent with a focal surgical vessel than diffuse microvascular coagulopathy. The tube pattern should prompt immediate surgical reassessment, imaging when it does not delay control, and early return to the operating room if bleeding remains significant. Empiric product administration may transiently obscure the trajectory but cannot ligate an internal thoracic branch or chest-wall vessel.","reconcile-discordant"],
    ["d11-bleeding","A patient after complex arch surgery has diffuse oozing. ROTEM shows delayed clot initiation, low fibrin-specific firmness, low platelet contribution, and rapid clot lysis. Which strategy is most appropriate?",["Correct each demonstrated deficit in a prioritized, reassessed sequence while treating physiology and excluding surgical bleeding","Give every blood product simultaneously without repeat testing","Treat only fibrinolysis because it explains every abnormal parameter","Return to the operating room without correcting profound coagulopathy"],0,"This is a mixed coagulopathy rather than a single-product problem. Antifibrinolytic adequacy, fibrinogen, platelets, factor replacement, residual heparin, temperature, pH, and calcium should be corrected in a goal-directed sequence with repeat tracing and clinical assessment. Surgical and coagulopathic bleeding can coexist, so targeted resuscitation must occur alongside source evaluation. Blind component stacking increases volume and thrombosis without confirming correction.","dosed-algorithm"],
    ["d11-bleeding","During massive transfusion, a patient becomes hypotensive with a prolonged QT interval and ionized calcium 0.76 mmol/L. What is the most appropriate action?",["Administer intravenous calcium with serial ionized-calcium and ECG reassessment","Stop hemorrhage control and wait for citrate to metabolize","Give sodium bicarbonate as the only correction","Infuse more citrate-containing plasma to normalize calcium"],0,"Citrate in transfused blood products binds ionized calcium. Severe hypocalcemia impairs contractility, vascular tone, platelet function, and coagulation and can prolong the QT interval. Intravenous calcium should be titrated with repeat ionized levels and ECG monitoring while hemorrhage control continues. Waiting permits cardiovascular deterioration, and bicarbonate does not replace the missing biologically active calcium.","complication-rescue"],
    ["d11-bleeding","Two hours after plasma transfusion, a patient develops hypoxemia and bilateral infiltrates. CVP is 19 mm Hg, blood pressure is elevated, and bedside ultrasound shows a plethoric IVC. Which diagnosis is more likely?",["Transfusion-associated circulatory overload","Transfusion-related acute lung injury","Anaphylaxis without pulmonary edema","Acute hemolysis limited to the lungs"],0,"Hypertension, elevated filling pressure, and venous congestion favor hydrostatic edema from transfusion-associated circulatory overload. Stop the transfusion, provide oxygen and ventilatory support, and use diuresis when perfusion permits. TRALI produces noncardiogenic permeability edema and lacks clear circulatory overload, although mixed pictures occur. Urticaria or hemoglobinuria would point toward different transfusion reactions.","reconcile-discordant"],
    ["d11-bleeding","A stable postoperative patient has hemoglobin 74 g/L, no active bleeding, normal lactate, and no ischemic ECG change. What transfusion approach best limits avoidable exposure?",["Use a restrictive strategy with clinical assessment and single-unit reassessment if transfusion is chosen","Transfuse automatically to hemoglobin 120 g/L because the patient had CPB","Give four units together to prevent future anemia","Withhold red cells even if myocardial ischemia or tissue hypoxia develops"],0,"In stable cardiac-surgery patients without active ischemia, restrictive red-cell strategies and single-unit reassessment reduce unnecessary transfusion without sacrificing outcomes. The threshold is not a substitute for symptoms, oxygen delivery, ongoing bleeding, and coronary risk. A fixed liberal target exposes the patient to TACO, reactions, and immunologic complications, while an absolute prohibition ignores evolving tissue hypoxia.","when-not-to"],
    ["d11-bleeding","After CPB, clot initiation and platelet contribution are adequate, but fibrin-specific firmness remains low after one standard cryoprecipitate dose. Bleeding continues. What should guide the next step?",["Repeat fibrinogen or viscoelastic assessment and dose further replacement to the measured deficit","Assume the first dose must have corrected fibrinogen and ignore the tracing","Switch immediately to platelets because cryoprecipitate contains none","Give protamine until fibrin firmness rises"],0,"Product effect varies with starting fibrinogen, blood volume, consumption, and the dose delivered. Persistent low fibrin-based firmness requires repeat measurement and further concentrated replacement according to a protocol, while ongoing surgical loss and physiology are addressed. Platelets cannot correct an isolated fibrinogen deficit, and protamine affects heparin rather than the substrate required for fibrin polymerization.","complication-rescue"],
    ["d11-bleeding","A patient taking ticagrelor requires urgent CABG for refractory ischemia. Platelet-function testing shows profound P2Y12 inhibition and surgery cannot be delayed. Which preparation is most defensible?",["Anticipate bleeding, use meticulous hemostasis and antifibrinolysis, and arrange targeted blood-component support","Assume one prophylactic platelet dose completely neutralizes circulating ticagrelor","Cancel revascularization regardless of ongoing ischemia","Give vitamin K because it reverses P2Y12 inhibition"],0,"Urgency must be balanced against known antiplatelet effect. When ischemia prevents delay, the team should plan surgical technique, blood conservation, antifibrinolytic therapy, cell salvage, and targeted component support. Circulating ticagrelor can inhibit transfused platelets, so prophylactic transfusion is not a guaranteed antidote. Vitamin K reverses vitamin K antagonists, not platelet P2Y12 blockade.","when-not-to"],
    ["d11-bleeding","During redo aortic surgery, shed mediastinal blood is collected for cell salvage. Which situation makes reinfusion inappropriate without special processing or an alternative strategy?",["Gross contamination of the field with enteric contents","Heparinized blood from an otherwise clean surgical field","Moderate anemia during ongoing blood loss","Use of an antifibrinolytic earlier in the case"],0,"Cell salvage washes and concentrates red cells but does not sterilize grossly contaminated blood or remove every hazardous substance. Enteric contamination creates an unacceptable infectious burden and requires a different blood-conservation strategy. Properly managed heparinized shed blood is a routine indication for cell salvage, while anemia and ongoing loss strengthen rather than negate its usefulness.","when-not-to"],
    ["d11-bleeding","After reversal, a bleeding patient has a normal ACT, normal heparinase comparison, adequate fibrinogen, and platelets, but PT and aPTT are prolonged after massive crystalloid dilution. Which component best matches the deficit?",["Plasma for broad coagulation-factor replacement","Additional protamine despite no residual-heparin evidence","Cryoprecipitate for already adequate fibrinogen","Red cells to shorten PT and aPTT directly"],0,"Concordant prolongation of PT and aPTT with adequate fibrinogen and platelet contribution suggests broad soluble-factor depletion. Plasma provides multiple coagulation factors and is appropriate when bleeding is clinically significant. The normal heparin evaluation argues against more protamine. Cryoprecipitate is concentrated fibrinogen, and red cells restore oxygen carriage rather than coagulation-factor activity.","reconcile-discordant"],

    ["d11-reexplore","Three hours after CABG, chest-tube output abruptly falls to zero while MAP falls, CVP rises, and pulse pressure narrows. The patient is too unstable for transport. What should the team prioritize?",["Immediate bedside echocardiographic assessment and preparation for urgent chest reopening","Document successful hemostasis and continue routine observation","Remove the tubes and delay imaging until morning","Give repeated fluid boluses without evaluating obstruction"],0,"Abrupt cessation of drainage during evolving obstructive shock suggests clotted tubes and retained blood rather than control of bleeding. Focused imaging may demonstrate chamber compression but must not delay decompression in a crashing patient. Fluids and vasoactive support are temporary bridges. Delay allows localized clot to produce tamponade even without a large circumferential effusion.","complication-rescue"],
    ["d11-reexplore","A post-CABG patient arrests in the ICU from suspected tamponade. External compressions and standard resuscitation are ineffective, and a trained resternotomy team is present. What is the next rescue principle?",["Perform emergency resternotomy and internal cardiac resuscitation according to the unit protocol","Continue external compressions indefinitely because reopening is never appropriate outside the operating room","Transfer to CT before opening the chest","Administer thrombolysis for presumed graft thrombosis"],0,"After recent sternotomy, reversible causes such as tamponade, severe bleeding, graft compression, and pacing failure require a cardiac-surgery arrest pathway. When trained staff and equipment are available, emergency resternotomy permits clot evacuation, internal massage, pacing, defibrillation, and direct control. Transport or prolonged ineffective external CPR delays correction of a surgically reversible arrest.","dosed-algorithm"],
    ["d11-reexplore","Persistent bleeding after valve surgery is accompanied by a normal viscoelastic profile and a new expanding mediastinal shadow on serial radiographs. What is the best interpretation?",["A focal surgical bleed with retained mediastinal blood is likely","Normal coagulation excludes clinically important bleeding","The radiographic change proves isolated pulmonary edema","More plasma should be given before any surgical review"],0,"Normal clot formation does not exclude a loose suture, cannulation-site bleed, or injured vessel. An enlarging mediastinal collection plus ongoing blood loss localizes a surgical problem and raises the risk of tamponade. Early operative reassessment is safer than empiric component therapy, which cannot control a focal source and may worsen congestion.","reconcile-discordant"],
    ["d11-reexplore","After LITA harvest, a patient has persistent left pleural drainage with normal coagulation. Re-exploration finds the graft intact. Which source should be sought carefully?",["An unclipped internal thoracic side branch or chest-wall vessel","An isolated right atrial cannulation tear causing only left pleural blood","Uremic platelet dysfunction despite normal focal findings","TACO confined to the left pleural space"],0,"The internal thoracic pedicle has multiple side branches that can retract and bleed into the pleural space, as can intercostal and chest-wall vessels injured during harvest. Localized left drainage with intact coagulation and an intact graft makes these sources more likely than systemic coagulopathy. Re-exploration should follow the entire harvest bed rather than inspecting only the anastomosis.","operative-step"],
    ["d11-reexplore","A patient returns for bleeding after ascending aortic replacement. There is brisk blood behind the graft near the posterior distal anastomosis, but exposure is limited. What operative principle is safest?",["Re-establish adequate support and exposure before attempting definitive suture repair","Place blind deep sutures through the graft and adjacent pulmonary artery","Pack indefinitely without identifying the source","Reverse all anticoagulation before securing perfusion support"],0,"Posterior aortic bleeding can be difficult to expose and lies near vulnerable structures. Safe repair requires hemodynamic control, adequate bypass or circulatory support when needed, evacuation of clot, and direct visualization before reinforcing the anastomosis. Blind sutures can injure the pulmonary artery or coronary structures, while packing alone may conceal ongoing high-pressure bleeding.","operative-step"],
    ["d11-reexplore","At re-exploration, diffuse oozing improves after warming and targeted products, but one jet persists from the right atrial cannulation purse-string. What is the definitive treatment?",["Direct surgical repair of the cannulation site","Additional plasma until the focal jet stops","High-dose recombinant factor VIIa as first-line therapy","Observation because diffuse bleeding improved"],0,"Once a discrete high-flow source is identified, hemostasis requires direct repair. Coagulation optimization helps surrounding tissue hold sutures but cannot close a mechanical defect. Powerful procoagulants expose fresh grafts and prostheses to thrombosis without replacing a loose purse-string. The field should be inspected after repair under representative pressure before closure.","operative-step"],
    ["d11-reexplore","A patient with low chest-tube output becomes anemic and hypoxemic. Ultrasound shows a large retained right hemothorax compressing the lung. What is the priority?",["Drain or evacuate the retained blood and identify the pleural bleeding source","Assume anemia is dilutional because mediastinal output is low","Increase PEEP without relieving the compressive collection","Give diuretics as definitive treatment"],0,"Chest-tube numbers can underestimate bleeding when tubes are clotted or blood accumulates outside the drained mediastinum. A large compressive hemothorax impairs ventilation and conceals blood loss. Prompt drainage or operative evacuation with source control is required. Higher airway pressure may worsen hemodynamics, and diuresis cannot remove organized pleural blood.","complication-rescue"],
    ["d11-reexplore","During re-exploration for bleeding, the surgeon finds a small right ventricular laceration adherent to the sternum. Which principle best limits further injury?",["Gain controlled exposure and decompression, then repair with carefully placed pledgeted sutures","Pull the sternum apart rapidly to improve visualization","Apply blind clamps to the beating right ventricle","Pack the laceration and close immediately despite ongoing bleeding"],0,"The thin-walled right ventricle is vulnerable during redo entry and may tear further with traction. Controlled exposure, decompression on support when necessary, gentle handling, and directly visualized pledgeted repair reduce extension and coronary injury. Blind clamping or hurried spreading can enlarge the defect; packing may temporize but is not definitive for active ventricular bleeding.","operative-step"],
    ["d11-reexplore","After valve surgery, a temporary pacing wire site bleeds briskly when the wire is removed. The patient becomes hypotensive and TEE shows pericardial blood. What should occur?",["Urgent surgical assessment for wire-related myocardial injury and tamponade","Apply pressure only at the skin exit and discharge the patient","Give thrombolysis to prevent clot around the wire tract","Insert another wire through the same tract without imaging"],0,"Temporary wires can rarely tear myocardium or a coronary branch during removal. Hypotension and new pericardial blood identify internal bleeding and possible tamponade, which skin pressure cannot control. Immediate imaging, resuscitation, and operative readiness are required. Anticoagulation or blind reinsertion can worsen the injury.","complication-rescue"],
    ["d11-reexplore","A patient re-explored for bleeding has no focal source, but the sternum and marrow surfaces ooze diffusely despite normalized coagulation. Which local maneuver is reasonable?",["Use directed bone-wax or topical hemostatic measures while avoiding coronary and graft compression","Pack large hemostatic material directly around every graft without planned removal","Close the chest under tension to tamponade the bleeding","Apply electrocautery to a bypass graft surface"],0,"After systemic deficits are corrected, controlled local hemostatic measures can address cancellous sternal or raw-surface bleeding. They must be placed selectively so they do not compress grafts, embolize, impair healing, or conceal ongoing surgical bleeding. Tension closure and indiscriminate packing risk tamponade and graft obstruction, while graft cautery can cause catastrophic injury.","operative-step"],
    ["d11-reexplore","A patient is returned early for high drainage. At re-exploration, a bleeding saphenous-vein graft side branch is clipped and the field becomes dry. What should be checked before reclosure?",["Graft lie and flow, all cannulation and anastomotic sites, and hemostasis at representative pressure","Only the clipped branch because a single source excludes every other lesion","Chest-tube patency after the sternum has already been closed","Coagulation tests tomorrow rather than intraoperative flow"],0,"Finding one source does not eliminate another, and manipulation during re-entry can kink or injure a graft. Before closure, the team should systematically inspect the field, verify graft lie and flow, challenge hemostasis at an appropriate pressure, and ensure drains are patent and positioned. A disciplined reinspection prevents a second avoidable return to the operating room.","operative-step"],
    ["d11-reexplore","A re-exploration candidate has profound hypothermia, pH 7.05, and uncontrolled diffuse bleeding, but bedside imaging also shows a rapidly enlarging localized clot compressing the right atrium. How should priorities be combined?",["Correct lethal physiology during immediate preparation for surgical decompression and source control","Normalize every laboratory value before addressing chamber compression","Operate without warming, calcium, or transfusion support","Treat only coagulopathy because a localized clot cannot cause shock"],0,"Surgical obstruction and systemic coagulopathy can coexist. The expanding clot requires urgent decompression, while warming, calcium, acid-base correction through perfusion restoration, and targeted products proceed in parallel so repair can hold. Waiting for perfect laboratory values permits obstructive collapse, whereas ignoring physiology makes recurrent bleeding and cardiac failure more likely.","dosed-algorithm"],
    ["d11-reexplore","At re-exploration after CABG, a posterior pericardial clot compresses the left atrium. Why may the usual textbook signs of tamponade be absent?",["Postoperative adhesions and localized clot can compress one chamber without circumferential pressure","Tamponade always requires right-ventricular diastolic collapse","A left-atrial clot cannot affect ventricular filling","Mechanical ventilation permanently prevents tamponade physiology"],0,"After surgery, adhesions partition the pericardial space, so blood can form a regional high-pressure collection that selectively compresses the left atrium or a ventricle. TTE may miss posterior clot, and classic pulsus or right-sided collapse may be absent. Hemodynamics and TEE should be integrated, with operative evacuation when organized clot causes shock.","reconcile-discordant"],
    ["d11-reexplore","A patient develops recurrent bleeding after an initially dry re-exploration. ACT is now prolonged and a heparinase channel corrects the initiation time. What mechanism is most likely?",["Delayed heparin rebound from redistribution after protamine","New platelet production failure occurring within minutes","Factor XIII excess causing clot breakdown","Aortic graft infection developing during the procedure"],0,"Heparin can redistribute from tissue compartments after the initial protamine-heparin complex clears, producing delayed anticoagulant activity and recurrent oozing. A paired heparinase tracing supports this specific diagnosis, permitting carefully titrated additional protamine with repeat assessment. It should not be inferred from ACT alone because hypothermia and dilution also prolong clotting.","reconcile-discordant"],
    ["d11-reexplore","After control of surgical bleeding, the open-chest patient develops severe RV edema and cannot tolerate sternal approximation without hypotension. What is the best closure strategy?",["Use temporary open-chest management with sterile coverage and planned delayed closure","Force immediate tight closure because an open chest is always fatal","Resect the right ventricle to create space","Remove all drains before temporary closure"],0,"If sternal approximation compresses an edematous or mechanically supported heart and causes hemodynamic collapse, delayed closure can preserve filling and output while edema and support requirements improve. Sterile temporary coverage, infection prevention, drainage, sedation, and a planned return are essential. Forced closure recreates obstructive physiology; abandoning drains or tissue is not a solution.","when-not-to"]
  ];

  rows.forEach((row, index) => window.QUESTIONS.push({
    id: `q-d11-${String(36 + index).padStart(2, "0")}`, day: 11, lesson: row[0], topic: "Hemostasis",
    stem: `<p>${row[1]}</p>`, options: row[2], answer: row[3], explain: `<p>${row[4]}</p>`,
    kind: row[5], level: index % 3 === 1 ? 5 : 4
  }));

  const strip = value => String(value || "").replace(/<[^>]*>/g, " ").replace(/&[^;]+;/g, " ").replace(/\s+/g, " ").trim();
  const clip = (value, count = 14) => strip(value).split(/\s+/).slice(0, count).join(" ");
  const esc = value => String(value).replace(/[&<>"']/g, ch => ({"&":"&amp;","<":"&lt;",">":"&gt;",'"':"&quot;","'":"&#39;"})[ch]);
  window.QUESTIONS.filter(q => /^q-d11-/.test(q.id)).forEach(q => {
    if (!q.kind) q.kind = /next|response|management|treatment|should|therapy|action|complication/i.test(strip(q.stem)) ? "complication-rescue" : "reconcile-discordant";
    if (!q.level) q.level = 4;
    q.refs = Array.isArray(q.refs) && q.refs.length ? q.refs : ["cohn-5e", "key-questions-2e"];
    q.objective = q.objective || `Apply ${clip(q.options[q.answer], 14)} using clinical, laboratory, and operative clues in cardiac-surgery hemostasis decisions.`;
    const alternatives = q.options.filter((_, i) => i !== q.answer).map(option => `“${esc(clip(option, 10))}” does not correct the demonstrated bleeding mechanism.`).join(" ");
    q.explain = String(q.explain || "").replace(/<\/p>\s*$/, "") + `</p><p><strong>Why the other approaches fail:</strong> ${alternatives} <strong>Educational takeaway:</strong> Distinguish focal surgical bleeding from physiologic and coagulation defects, treat the measured cause, and reassess before adding another product or delaying source control.</p>`;
  });
})();

